return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Diagnostic Framework & Intellectual Disabilities',
        'slug' => 'diagnostic-framework-intellectual-disabilities',
        'description' => 'DSM-5 developmental timing criteria, adaptive functioning, and intellectual disability severity specifiers.',
        'overview' => "Neurodevelopmental disorders represent a clinically significant cluster of conditions characterized by developmental deficits that produce impairments of personal, social, academic, or occupational functioning. Typically manifesting early in development—often prior to grade school entry—these disorders arise from complex interactions between genetic vulnerabilities, prenatal neurological insults, and critical neurodevelopmental maturational windows.\n\nUnder the DSM-5 framework, Intellectual Disability (Intellectual Developmental Disorder) is conceptualized as a disorder with onset during the developmental period that includes both intellectual and adaptive functioning deficits in conceptual, social, and practical domains. A pivotal conceptual evolution from DSM-IV to DSM-5 is the decisive shift away from rigid psychometric intelligence quotient (IQ) cutoff scores (historically IQ < 70) as the sole determining factor of severity. Instead, DSM-5 places paramount diagnostic weight on comprehensive clinical assessment of adaptive functioning across everyday life environments.\n\nThis diagnostic paradigm recognizes that clinical impairment and everyday support needs are governed primarily by adaptive capabilities rather than abstract psychometric IQ metrics alone. Consequently, clinicians must evaluate an individual's capacity to navigate real-world demands, including communication, self-direction, social interpersonal engagement, personal hygiene, and independent living tasks.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "The diagnosis of Intellectual Disability requires the satisfaction of three core criteria: Criterion A mandates deficits in general mental abilities, such as reasoning, problem-solving, planning, abstract thinking, judgment, academic learning, and learning from experience. These cognitive deficits must be confirmed through both comprehensive clinical evaluation and individualized, standardized, psychometrically valid intelligence testing.\n\nCriterion B requires deficits in adaptive functioning that result in failure to meet developmental and sociocultural standards for personal independence and social responsibility. Without ongoing external support, these adaptive limitations impair functioning in one or more activities of daily life across multiple environments (home, school, work, and community) in at least one of three domains: conceptual, social, or practical.\n\nCriterion C establishes that the onset of all intellectual and adaptive deficits must occur during the developmental period. Onset occurring outside the developmental window (e.g., following traumatic brain injury or stroke in adulthood) is classified under Neurocognitive Disorders rather than Intellectual Disability.",
        'specifiers' => "Severity for Intellectual Disability is specified as Mild, Moderate, Severe, or Profound, determined strictly by adaptive functioning levels across conceptual, social, and practical domains rather than numerical IQ scores. This classification reflects the practical reality that adaptive functioning dictates the level of educational, residential, and occupational supports an individual requires.\n\nIn Mild Intellectual Disability, individuals may acquire academic skills up to late elementary levels and maintain concrete social interactions, often achieving independent community living with minimal guidance. Conversely, Profound Intellectual Disability involves severe limitations in all domains; individuals depend entirely on others for basic physical self-care, health maintenance, and safety, communicating primarily through nonverbal gestures or emotional cues.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "A primary differential diagnosis is Major and Mild Neurocognitive Disorders. While both involve cognitive compromise, Neurocognitive Disorders are distinguished by a clinically documented loss of previously acquired cognitive functions following adulthood, whereas Intellectual Disability represents an early developmental failure to acquire expected milestones.\n\nSpecific Learning Disorder must also be differentiated from Intellectual Disability. In Specific Learning Disorder, academic difficulties are circumscribed to specific learning domains (such as reading decoding, written expression, or mathematical calculations), while general intellectual reasoning and broad adaptive functioning in non-academic spheres remain intact. Global Developmental Delay is diagnosed exclusively in children under the age of 5 years when systematic standardized psychometric testing cannot be reliably administered.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 7-year-old child referred by educators due to pronounced academic frustration and difficulties navigating playground interactions. Psychometric evaluation indicates significant delays in abstract logic and quantitative reasoning alongside prominent deficits in independent dressing, personal grooming, and conversational turn-taking.\n\nComprehensive assessment reveals that these functional delays were present since toddlerhood. Because the child demonstrates marked adaptive limitations across practical and social spheres alongside cognitive delays during the developmental period, the clinical formulation confirms a diagnosis of Mild Intellectual Disability, highlighting the necessity of an individualized education program (IEP) focused on adaptive self-reliance.",
        'summary_notes' => "Key board examination takeaways: DSM-5 establishes adaptive functioning—not numerical IQ score cutoffs—as the primary criterion determining severity specifiers for Intellectual Disability. The developmental period onset requirement is essential for excluding acquired adult neurocognitive conditions. Global Developmental Delay remains a temporary diagnostic classification reserved for pediatric patients under age 5 whose clinical severity cannot be definitively measured with standardized instruments.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Adaptive Functioning Priority', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Severity levels (Mild to Profound) are determined by adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, and practical domains rather than IQ numbers alone.'],
            ['title' => 'Developmental Period Onset', 'icon' => '🌱', 'definition' => 'Intellectual and adaptive deficits must manifest during childhood or adolescence; adult-onset cognitive decline is classified under Neurocognitive Disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'Tri-Domain Adaptive Assessment', 'icon' => '🌐', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive clinical evaluation evaluates Conceptual (academic/logic), Social (empathy/communication), and Practical (daily self-care/safety) capabilities.'],
            ['title' => 'Global Developmental Delay', 'icon' => '⏳', 'definition' => 'A diagnostic classification reserved strictly for children younger than 5 years when severity cannot be reliably measured via formal testing.']
