    2 => [
        'title' => 'Communication Disorders',
        'slug' => 'communication-disorders',
        'description' => 'Language Disorder, Speech Sound Disorder, Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder (Stuttering), and Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder.',
        'overview' => "Communication disorders represent a specialized diagnostic category within neurodevelopmental disorders encompassing disturbances in speech production, language acquisition, and social pragmatic communication. Effective communication requires the seamless integration of central linguistic processing, neurological motor coordination, and interpersonal social comprehension; failure within any of these pathways leads to distinct clinical presentations.\n\nA fundamental advancement in the DSM-5 taxonomy was the introduction of Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder (SPCD). This diagnosis addresses individuals who demonstrate profound impairments in the functional, social use of language—such as understanding non-literal idioms, maintaining conversational turn-taking, and modulating vocal register according to audience—in the complete absence of the repetitive, restricted behaviors characteristic of Autism Spectrum Disorder.\n\nDistinguishing between structural speech production difficulties and broader symbolic linguistic formulation is central to accurate differential diagnosis. Speech sound disorder involves the mechanical execution of phonemes, whereas language disorder reflects compromised acquisition and utilization of spoken, written, or sign-language symbol systems across expressive and receptive modalities.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Language Disorder requires persistent difficulties in the acquisition and use of language across modalities (spoken, written, sign language) due to deficits in comprehension or production, marked by reduced vocabulary, limited sentence structure, and impairments in discourse, falling substantially below age expectations and causing functional limitations.\n\nSpeech Sound Disorder involves persistent difficulty with speech sound production that interferes with speech intelligibility or prevents verbal communication. Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder (Stuttering) involves disturbances in the normal fluency and time patterning of speech (frequent sound prolongations, repetitions, broken words, or audible pauses) that cause anxiety about speaking.\n\nSocial (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder (SPCD) mandates persistent difficulties in the social use of verbal and nonverbal communication (Criterion A), leading to functional limitations (Criterion B), with onset in the early developmental period (Criterion C), and crucially, symptoms must not be better explained by low abilities in the domains of structural language or Autism Spectrum Disorder (Criterion D).",
        'specifiers' => "Communication disorders can be specified according to whether they are associated with a known medical, neurological, or sensory condition (such as hearing impairment or craniofacial abnormalities). In Speech Sound Disorder, clinicians specify whether phonological processing deficits or motor speech articulation deficits predominate.\n\nFor Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder, clinicians evaluate whether secondary physical motor accompaniments are present, such as eye blinks, facial tics, head jerks, or vocal tremors that emerge during intense speech blocks, reflecting excessive physiological struggle.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "The primary differential boundary lies between Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder (SPCD) and Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD). If an individual exhibits pragmatic communication deficits accompanied by restricted interests, rigid adherence to routines, sensory hypo/hyperactivity, or stereotyped motor movements, the diagnosis must be Autism Spectrum Disorder; SPCD cannot be diagnosed if Criterion B of ASD has ever been met.\n\nSelective Mutism must also be excluded. In Selective Mutism, the child possesses normal linguistic competence and speaks fluently in select secure settings (such as at home with parents), but demonstrates a consistent, anxiety-driven failure to speak in specific social situations (such as the classroom).",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves an 8-year-old student who exhibits difficulty engaging with peers during group discussions. The student interprets metaphors literally, frequently interrupts speakers without recognizing conversational pauses, and speaks in an overly formal, pedantic register on the playground.\n\nComprehensive assessment reveals intact grammatical syntax and age-appropriate vocabulary. Thorough developmental history reveals no history of sensory hypersensitivity, hand flapping, or inflexible routines. The absence of restricted, repetitive behaviors rules out Autism Spectrum Disorder, confirming a diagnosis of Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls: Stuttering is formally designated as Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder in DSM-5. Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder was specifically added to classify pragmatic communication deficits that lack the restricted, repetitive behavioral patterns required for an ASD diagnosis. Selective Mutism reflects situation-specific anxiety rather than a primary deficit in language acquisition.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Structural vs. Pragmatic Deficits', 'icon' => '🗣️', 'definition' => 'Language Disorder affects vocabulary and syntactic grammar, whereas Pragmatic Disorder affects social context, tone, and conversational etiquette.'],
            ['title' => 'Exclusion of Restricted Behaviors', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder requires the total absence of restricted, repetitive behaviors or stereotypies characteristic of ASD.'],
            ['title' => 'Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder', 'icon' => '🌊', 'definition' => 'Characterized by sound/syllable repetitions, prolongations, broken words, and audible pauses that disrupt normal speech rhythm.'],
            ['title' => 'Speech Sound Production', 'icon' => '🎙️', 'definition' => 'Involves mechanical difficulty articulating phonemes clearly, resulting in compromised speech intelligibility despite intact conceptual language.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Pragmatics', 'definition' => 'The social rules and contextual conventions governing communicative interactions, including conversational turn-taking, idiom decoding, and adjusting tone to the listener.'],
            ['term' => 'Phonological Processing', 'definition' => 'The cognitive and motor ability to mentally categorize, organize, and physically articulate distinct speech sounds into intelligible spoken words.'],
            ['term' => 'Discourse Impairment', 'definition' => 'Difficulty connecting sentences to describe or explain a sequence of events, convey a cohesive narrative, or sustain a two-way dialogue.'],
            ['term' => 'Selective Mutism', 'definition' => 'A distinct anxiety disorder characterized by persistent failure to speak in specific social situations where speech is expected despite speaking normally elsewhere.']
        ]
    ],
    3 => [
        'title' => 'Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)',
        'slug' => 'autism-spectrum-disorder',
        'description' => 'DSM-5 dimensional criteria, social-emotional reciprocity deficits, restricted repetitive patterns, and severity level grading.',
        'overview' => "Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) represents a neurodevelopmental condition characterized by persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, accompanied by restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or activities. In a historic revision, DSM-5 collapsed previously separate categorical diagnoses—including Autistic Disorder, Asperger's Disorder, and Pervasive Developmental Disorder Not Otherwise Specified (PDD-NOS)—into a single dimensional spectrum.\n\nThis unified dimensional framework reflects extensive neuroscientific and clinical evidence demonstrating that these historical categories shared core biological and behavioral characteristics that varied along a continuum of severity, cognitive ability, and linguistic development rather than representing distinct pathophysiological entities. The clinical presentation of ASD is remarkably heterogeneous, spanning from nonverbal individuals with severe intellectual impairment to highly articulate individuals with subtle social idiosyncrasies.\n\nUnderlying the disorder are complex alterations in neural connectivity, particularly involving frontostriatal circuits, the default mode network, and mirror neuron systems that mediate social cognition, joint attention, and cognitive flexibility. Symptoms typically manifest during the second year of life as social communication demands begin to exceed limited capacities, though subtle presentations may remain masked until adolescence or adulthood.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnosis mandates the satisfaction of all criteria across two primary symptom domains. Criterion A requires persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, manifested by all three of the following: (1) deficits in social-emotional reciprocity (ranging from abnormal social approach to total lack of shared affect); (2) deficits in nonverbal communicative behaviors used for social interaction (abnormalities in eye contact, body language, or total lack of facial expressions); and (3) deficits in developing, maintaining, and understanding relationships.\n\nCriterion B requires restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or activities, manifested by at least two of the following four: (1) stereotyped or repetitive motor movements, use of objects, or speech (echolalia, hand flapping, lining up toys); (2) insistence on sameness, inflexible adherence to routines, or ritualized patterns; (3) highly restricted, fixated interests that are abnormal in intensity or focus; and (4) hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input or unusual interest in sensory aspects of the environment.\n\nCriterion C establishes that symptoms must be present in the early developmental period (though they may not become fully manifest until social demands exceed limited capacities). Criterion D requires clinically significant impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning. Criterion E dictates that these disturbances are not better explained by intellectual disability alone.",
        'specifiers' => "Severity is graded independently for Criterion A (social communication) and Criterion B (restricted behaviors) into three distinct support tiers: Level 1 ('Requiring support'), Level 2 ('Requiring substantial support'), and Level 3 ('Requiring very substantial support'). These levels provide a standardized description of the current level of functional assistance necessary.\n\nCrucial diagnostic specifiers must also be recorded: 'With or without accompanying intellectual impairment', 'With or without accompanying language impairment', 'Associated with a known medical or genetic condition or environmental factor' (such as Fragile X syndrome or tuberous sclerosis), and 'Associated with another neurodevelopmental, mental, or behavioral disorder'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "The primary differential includes Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder (SPCD). If an individual exhibits social communication deficits but fails to meet at least two criteria in Domain B (restricted/repetitive behaviors), ASD is excluded and SPCD must be considered.\n\nAttention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) is a frequent comorbid condition; individuals with ADHD may show social awkwardness secondary to impulsivity or inattention, but they do not exhibit the fundamental deficits in joint attention and restricted repetitive rituals required for ASD. Both conditions may be diagnosed concurrently under DSM-5 if criteria for each are met. Reactive Attachment Disorder involves severely disturbed social relatedness arising from pathogenic early care, but restricted interests and sensory atypicalities are absent.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical scenario involves a 4-year-old child who rarely makes eye contact, fails to respond when called by name, and does not point to share enjoyment with parents (joint attention deficit). When distressed, the child spins wheels on toy cars for hours, insists on identical driving routes to preschool, and exhibits intense distress when exposed to humming fluorescent lights.\n\nAssessment reveals all three Criterion A social reciprocity deficits alongside three Criterion B indicators (repetitive object use, insistence on sameness, sensory hyperreactivity). The child is diagnosed with Autism Spectrum Disorder, Level 2 ('Requiring substantial support') for social communication and Level 2 for restricted, repetitive behaviors, with accompanying language impairment.",
        'summary_notes' => "Essential board examination pearls: DSM-5 combined Autistic Disorder and Asperger's Disorder into a single dimensional ASD diagnosis. Diagnosis requires all 3 criteria in Domain A (social communication) and at least 2 of 4 in Domain B (repetitive behaviors/sensory atypicality). Hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input was formally added to Criterion B in DSM-5. ADHD and ASD can now be formally co-diagnosed, reversing the previous DSM-IV prohibition.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Unified Dimensional Spectrum', 'icon' => '🌈', 'definition' => 'Replaced categorical subtypes (Autistic Disorder, Asperger syndrome, PDD-NOS) with a dimensional continuum graded by support levels.'],
            ['title' => 'Criterion A: Complete Requirement', 'icon' => '🤝', 'definition' => 'Mandates deficits in all three social areas: social-emotional reciprocity, nonverbal communication, and developing/maintaining relationships.'],
            ['title' => 'Criterion B: Behavioral Threshold', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Requires at least 2 of 4 features: motor stereotypies, insistence on sameness/routines, fixated interests, and sensory hyper/hypo-reactivity.'],
            ['title' => 'Support Level Tiers', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Levels 1 (Requiring support), 2 (Requiring substantial support), and 3 (Requiring very substantial support) classified separately for both domains.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Social-Emotional Reciprocity', 'definition' => 'The ability to engage in back-and-forth social interaction, share emotions, initiate conversations, and respond appropriately to others.'],
            ['term' => 'Joint Attention', 'definition' => 'The shared focus of two individuals on an object, achieved via eye-gaze, pointing, or gestures, which forms the developmental bedrock of human social communication.'],
            ['term' => 'Stereotypy', 'definition' => 'Repetitive, non-functional motor behaviors such as hand flapping, body rocking, spinning, or vocal echolalia.'],
            ['term' => 'Sensory Reactivity', 'definition' => 'Atypical neurological responsiveness to sensory stimuli, manifesting as extreme distress toward sounds/textures or fascination with visual motion/lights.']
        ]
    ],
    4 => [
        'title' => 'Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)',
        'slug' => 'adhd-criteria-subtypes',
        'description' => 'Core symptom clusters, developmental age thresholds, presentation specifiers, and executive dysfunction.',
        'overview' => "Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) is a persistent neurodevelopmental syndrome characterized by impairing levels of inattention, disorganization, and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that are inconsistent with developmental level. Symptoms stem from dysregulation within frontostriatal dopamine and norepinephrine neurotransmission systems, resulting in pervasive executive dysfunction affecting working memory, inhibitory control, cognitive flexibility, and temporal organization.\n\nIn DSM-5, critical updates were implemented to reflect developmental progression across the lifespan. Notably, the age-of-onset threshold was raised from symptoms occurring prior to age 7 to onset of multiple inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms prior to age 12. This revision addresses clinical and epidemiological evidence that inattentive symptoms are frequently recognized only when structured academic and organizational demands escalate in late childhood.\n\nFurthermore, recognizing that hyperactivity frequently diminishes into internal subjective restlessness in adulthood, DSM-5 adjusted the symptom threshold for mature individuals. While children require at least 6 symptoms in either domain, individuals aged 17 and older require only 5 symptoms, ensuring appropriate diagnostic sensitivity across adult clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires a persistent pattern of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that interferes with functioning or development. For Inattention, at least 6 symptoms (or 5 for individuals aged 17 and older) must have persisted for at least 6 months to a degree inconsistent with developmental level: careless errors, difficulty sustaining attention, appearing not to listen, failure to finish tasks, disorganization, avoidance of sustained mental effort, losing items, high distractibility, and daily forgetfulness.\n\nFor Hyperactivity and Impulsivity, at least 6 symptoms (or 5 for age 17+) must have persisted for at least 6 months: fidgeting, leaving seat inappropriately, running/climbing inappropriately (or subjective restlessness in teens/adults), unable to play quietly, 'on the go' as if driven by a motor, excessive talking, blurting out answers, difficulty awaiting turn, and interrupting or intruding on others.\n\nCriterion B requires that several inattentive or hyperactive-impulsive symptoms were present prior to age 12 years. Criterion C mandates that several symptoms are present in two or more settings (e.g., at home, school, or work; with friends or relatives; in other activities). Criterion D requires clear evidence of interference with social, academic, or occupational functioning. Criterion E dictates that symptoms do not occur exclusively during a psychotic disorder and are not better explained by another mental disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify the current presentation based on the predominant symptom profile over the past 6 months: Combined Presentation (both inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity criteria are met), Predominantly Inattentive Presentation (inattention criteria met, but hyperactivity-impulsivity not fully met), or Predominantly Hyperactive/Impulsive Presentation (hyperactivity-impulsivity criteria met, but inattention not fully met).\n\nAdditionally, clinicians specify 'In partial remission' when full criteria were previously met, fewer than full criteria have been met for the past 6 months, and symptoms still result in functional impairment. Current severity is coded as Mild, Moderate, or Severe based on symptom count and degree of functional impairment.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD) frequently co-occurs with ADHD. In ODD, failure to follow instructions stems from active refusal, defiance, and hostility, whereas in ADHD, failure to complete tasks is driven by inattention, working memory deficits, and distractibility rather than spite.\n\nSpecific Learning Disorder must be evaluated; a child with a learning disorder may display inattention solely during tasks involving their specific academic deficit (e.g., reading), whereas ADHD inattention is pervasive across diverse environments and non-academic activities. Bipolar Disorder involves episodic mood elevation, grandiosity, and decreased need for sleep, contrasting with the chronic, non-episodic motor and attentional dysregulation characteristic of ADHD.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical evaluation involves a 10-year-old child whose teacher reports chronic daydreaming, incomplete classroom assignments, and losing essential homework folders. At home, the parents report having to repeat verbal directions five times and finding the child easily distracted by ambient household noises.\n\nDevelopmental records demonstrate that attentional difficulties emerged by age 8. The child exhibits no marked motor restlessness or impulsive interruptions. The clinical picture meets 7 inattention criteria across both home and school environments, confirming ADHD, Predominantly Inattentive Presentation, Moderate Severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The DSM-5 onset threshold requires several symptoms to be present prior to age 12 (expanded from age 7 in DSM-IV). The symptom threshold drops from 6 to 5 symptoms for individuals aged 17 and older. Symptoms must be documented in two or more independent settings (such as home and school) to rule out situational behavioral reactions. DSM-5 permits comorbid diagnosis of ADHD with Autism Spectrum Disorder.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Age of Onset: Prior to Age 12', 'icon' => '📅', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 raised the onset threshold from age 7 to age 12, acknowledging later identification of purely inattentive manifestations.'],
            ['title' => 'Multi-Setting Requirement', 'icon' => '🏫', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must manifest across two or more settings (e.g., school and home) to ensure systemic neurodevelopmental impairment.'],
            ['title' => 'Adult Symptom Threshold (5 Symptoms)', 'icon' => '🧑', 'definition' => 'Individuals aged 17 and older require only 5 symptoms in a domain (compared to 6 for children) to satisfy diagnostic thresholds.'],
            ['title' => 'Executive Dysfunction Core', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Impairments in prefrontal dopamine/norepinephrine pathways disrupt working memory, inhibitory control, and temporal planning.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Combined Presentation', 'definition' => 'Diagnostic specifier when both the full inattention threshold and the full hyperactivity-impulsivity threshold are met for the past 6 months.'],
            ['term' => 'Predominantly Inattentive', 'definition' => 'Specifier when inattention criteria are met but hyperactive-impulsive criteria fall below the diagnostic threshold.'],
            ['term' => 'Working Memory', 'definition' => 'The cognitive system responsible for temporarily holding and manipulating information necessary for completing complex tasks.'],
            ['term' => 'Inhibitory Control', 'definition' => 'The neurological capacity to suppress prepotent, automatic behavioral responses in order to pursue goal-directed actions.']
        ]
    ],
    5 => [
        'title' => 'Specific Learning Disorder',
        'slug' => 'specific-learning-disorder',
        'description' => 'Diagnostic criteria, reading (dyslexia), written expression, mathematics (dyscalculia), and RTI frameworks.',
        'overview' => "Specific Learning Disorder (SLD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder that impedes the ability to learn or use foundational academic skills in reading, written expression, or mathematics. In DSM-5, previously segregated conditions (Reading Disorder, Mathematics Disorder, Disorder of Written Expression) were integrated into a unified singular diagnosis with discrete domain specifiers.\n\nA fundamental diagnostic requirement is the '6-month persistent difficulty' rule: academic difficulties must persist for at least 6 months despite the provision of targeted educational interventions. This requirement ensures that transient educational gaps caused by instructional disruption, language barriers, or socioeconomic factors are not misattributed to an underlying neurobiological learning disorder.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 decisively abandoned the traditional IQ-achievement discrepancy model, which historically required a substantial statistical gap between an individual's IQ score and standardized academic achievement tests. By removing this requirement, DSM-5 prevents the problematic 'wait to fail' phenomenon and accommodates contemporary Response to Intervention (RTI) educational frameworks.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A requires difficulties learning and using academic skills, indicated by the presence of at least one of the following symptoms that have persisted for at least 6 months, despite targeted intervention: (1) inaccurate or slow and effortful word reading; (2) difficulty understanding the meaning of what is read; (3) difficulties with spelling; (4) difficulties with written expression; (5) difficulties mastering number sense, number facts, or calculation; (6) difficulties with mathematical reasoning.\n\nCriterion B mandates that the affected academic skills are substantially and quantifiably below those expected for chronological age, causing significant interference with academic or occupational performance, confirmed by individually administered standardized achievement measures and comprehensive clinical assessment.\n\nCriterion C establishes that learning difficulties begin during school-age years, but may not fully manifest until academic demands exceed the individual's limited capacities. Criterion D requires that learning difficulties are not better accounted for by intellectual disabilities, uncorrected visual or auditory acuity, other mental or neurological disorders, psychosocial adversity, lack of language proficiency, or inadequate educational instruction.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must specify all academic domains and subskills that are impaired: (1) With impairment in reading (specifying word reading accuracy, reading rate or fluency, and reading comprehension; commonly referred to as dyslexia); (2) With impairment in written expression (specifying spelling accuracy, grammar and punctuation accuracy, and clarity or organization of written expression); (3) With impairment in mathematics (specifying number sense, memorization of arithmetic facts, accurate or fluent calculation, and accurate math reasoning; commonly referred to as dyscalculia).\n\nSeverity is coded as Mild (difficulties in one or two academic domains, able to compensate with accommodations), Moderate (marked difficulties in one or more domains, requiring intensive, specialized instruction), or Severe (severe difficulties affecting multiple domains, requiring ongoing intensive individualized instruction for most of the school day).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Intellectual Disability must be distinguished from SLD. In Intellectual Disability, cognitive and academic delays are generalized across all cognitive spheres and accompanied by pervasive adaptive deficits; in SLD, general intellectual reasoning and everyday adaptive skills are intact, with cognitive deficits circumscribed to specific academic domains.\n\nSensory impairments (such as undiagnosed refractive visual errors or sensorineural hearing loss) must be ruled out through objective sensory screening. Neurodevelopmental ADHD must be distinguished: inattention in ADHD causes inconsistent academic performance across all subjects, whereas SLD causes specific, persistent difficulties in target academic domains even when the child is calm and focused.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves an 8-year-old third grader with average verbal reasoning who struggles profoundly with reading. The child reads aloud haltingly, reverses letter sequences, and guesses words based on initial phonemes. Despite six months of small-group phonics intervention, standardized reading testing places the child at the 4th percentile for word reading fluency, while mathematical calculation is at the 65th percentile.\n\nGeneral adaptive functioning is completely normal. The persistence of localized phonological decoding deficits despite intervention confirms a diagnosis of Specific Learning Disorder, With impairment in reading (Dyslexia), Moderate severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: DSM-5 eliminated the IQ-achievement discrepancy model in favor of the 6-month persistence rule despite targeted intervention. Dyslexia is an alternative term for SLD with impairment in reading; Dyscalculia is an alternative term for SLD with impairment in mathematics. General cognitive ability (IQ) remains intact outside the impaired academic domain.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => '6-Month Persistence Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Symptoms must persist for at least 6 months despite targeted educational intervention to exclude temporary instructional deficits.'],
            ['title' => 'Abandonment of Discrepancy Model', 'icon' => '❌', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 discarded the requirement of a statistical IQ-achievement discrepancy, facilitating early Response to Intervention (RTI) identification.'],
            ['title' => 'Domain-Specific Specifiers', 'icon' => '📚', 'definition' => 'Mandatory specifiers identify specific impairments: reading (dyslexia), written expression, and/or mathematics (dyscalculia).'],
            ['title' => 'Normal Intellectual Capacity', 'icon' => '💡', 'definition' => 'General mental ability and everyday adaptive functioning remain intact; impairment is circumscribed to academic processing.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Dyslexia', 'definition' => 'An alternative term referring to a pattern of learning difficulties characterized by problems with accurate or fluent word recognition, poor decoding, and weak spelling.'],
            ['term' => 'Dyscalculia', 'definition' => 'An alternative term referring to a pattern of difficulties characterized by problems processing numerical information, learning arithmetic facts, and performing accurate calculations.'],
            ['term' => 'Phonological Decoding', 'definition' => 'The cognitive linguistic process of translating written graphemes (letters) into their corresponding phonetic sounds to decipher unfamiliar words.'],
            ['term' => 'Response to Intervention (RTI)', 'definition' => 'A multi-tiered educational framework that provides increasingly intensive instructional support to evaluate whether learning difficulties persist.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Motor & Tic Disorders',
        'slug' => 'motor-and-tic-disorders',
        'description' => 'Tourette\'s Disorder, Persistent Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder, Provisional Tic Disorder, and Developmental Coordination Disorder.',
        'overview' => "Motor and Tic Disorders are neurodevelopmental conditions characterized by sudden, rapid, recurrent, nonrhythmic motor movements and/or vocalizations, or significant motor coordination impairments. The underlying pathophysiology involves dysfunction within cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loops, leading to diminished motor inhibition and excessive dopaminergic signaling in the basal ganglia.\n\nTics are uniquely experienced as having a premonitory urge—an internal, uncomfortable somatosensory tension preceding the tic that is temporarily relieved upon execution of the movement or sound. This sensory phenomena distinguishes tics from other involuntary movement disorders (such as chorea, dystonia, or myoclonus) and provides a crucial target for behavioral interventions such as Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT).\n\nUnder DSM-5, tic disorders are classified hierarchically based on the types of tics present (motor, vocal, or both) and their chronological duration. Tourette's Disorder sits at the pinnacle of this diagnostic hierarchy, requiring both multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic persisting for more than one year.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Tourette's Disorder requires: (1) both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics have been present at some time during the illness, though not necessarily concurrently; (2) tics may wax and wane in frequency but have persisted for more than 1 year since first tic onset; (3) onset is before age 18 years; (4) the disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., cocaine) or another medical condition (e.g., Huntington's disease, postviral encephalitis).\n\nPersistent (Chronic) Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder requires single or multiple motor or vocal tics, but NOT both motor and vocal; tics have persisted for more than 1 year; onset is before age 18; and criteria have NEVER been met for Tourette's Disorder.\n\nProvisional Tic Disorder is diagnosed when single or multiple motor and/or vocal tics have been present for LESS than 1 year since onset, with onset before age 18, and criteria have never been met for Tourette's or Persistent Tic Disorder.\n\nDevelopmental Coordination Disorder (DCD) requires motor coordination acquisition and execution substantially below chronological age, manifested by clumsiness, slowness, and inaccuracy, with onset in early development.",
        'specifiers' => "For Persistent Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder, clinicians must specify the presentation subtype: 'With motor tics only' or 'With vocal tics only'.\n\nFor Stereotypic Movement Disorder, clinicians specify whether repetitive, seemingly driven motor behaviors occur 'With self-injurious behavior' or 'Without self-injurious behavior', and whether the condition is associated with a known medical, genetic, or environmental condition.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Stereotypic Movement Disorder involves rhythmic, purposeful-appearing repetitive movements (hand flapping, body rocking) that lack premonitory urges and are not suppressed, whereas tics are sudden, nonrhythmic, and preceded by sensory tension.\n\nCompulsions in Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) are complex behaviors performed in response to an obsession or rigid rule to neutralize anxiety, whereas tics are semi-involuntary motor executions triggered by localized somatic urges. Substance-induced tics (such as stimulant or cocaine toxicity) and medical chorea (e.g., Sydenham chorea, Huntington disease) must be excluded.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical evaluation involves a 12-year-old child presenting with involuntary eye blinking, shoulder jerking, and throat clearing that began 18 months ago. The child reports an intense 'itch-like' sensation in the throat before clearing it, followed by brief relief.\n\nExamination confirms multiple motor tics (blinking, shoulder jerks) and a vocal tic (throat clearing) that have persisted across 18 months with onset during childhood. Because both motor and vocal tics have occurred over more than 1 year, the diagnostic formulation confirms Tourette's Disorder.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board examination points: Tourette's Disorder requires BOTH multiple motor tics AND at least one vocal tic persisting for more than 1 year, with onset prior to age 18. If only motor OR only vocal tics are present for >1 year, the diagnosis is Persistent (Chronic) Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder. If tics have been present for <1 year, the diagnosis is Provisional Tic Disorder. Tics wax and wane in severity and are preceded by premonitory sensory urges.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Diagnostic Hierarchy of Tics', 'icon' => '🪜', 'definition' => 'Tourette (motor + vocal, >1 yr) trumps Persistent Tic (motor OR vocal, >1 yr), which trumps Provisional Tic (<1 yr).'],
            ['title' => 'Premonitory Urges', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Sensory warning tensions preceding tic execution that are temporarily alleviated upon motor or vocal expression.'],
            ['title' => 'Onset Prior to Age 18', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'All primary tic disorders require onset prior to age 18; adult-onset tics necessitate investigation for secondary neurological disease.'],
            ['title' => 'Basal Ganglia Pathways', 'icon' => '🧠', 'definition' => 'Dysfunction in cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) motor circuits underlies diminished motor inhibition and tic generation.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Coprolalia', 'definition' => 'The involuntary vocalization of socially unacceptable or obscene words; present in fewer than 10-15% of Tourette patients despite media stereotypes.'],
            ['term' => 'Echolalia', 'definition' => 'The involuntary repetition or echoing of another person\'s vocalizations or words.'],
            ['term' => 'Echopraxia', 'definition' => 'The involuntary imitation or repetition of the movements or gestures of another individual.'],
            ['term' => 'Waning and Waxing', 'definition' => 'The characteristic natural course of tic disorders, marked by spontaneous fluctuations in frequency, intensity, and muscle group localization.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Neurodevelopmental Course & Etiology',
        'slug' => 'neurodevelopmental-course-etiology',
        'description' => 'Genetic architectures, synaptic pruning, epigenetic influences, and developmental trajectories.',
        'overview' => "The etiology of neurodevelopmental disorders is fundamentally multifactorial, rooted in polygenic inheritance, rare high-impact copy number variants (CNVs), and prenatal environmental perturbations during vulnerable periods of neurogenesis, neuronal migration, and synaptogenesis. Rather than arising from single isolated lesions, these conditions reflect disruptions in large-scale functional neural networks that govern executive processing, social cognition, and sensorimotor integration.\n\nDuring early brain development, the brain undergoes rapid synaptic proliferation followed by competitive synaptic pruning and myelination throughout childhood and adolescence. Abnormalities in pruning mechanisms—such as under-pruning in ASD resulting in local hyperconnectivity or disrupted frontostriatal connectivity in ADHD—alter the signal-to-noise ratio within cortical information-processing circuits.\n\nThe developmental course of neurodevelopmental disorders is characteristically chronic but dynamic. As children mature, clinical presentations evolve: overt motor hyperactivity in pediatric ADHD frequently transforms into internal subjective restlessness and executive disorganization in adulthood, while communication deficits in ASD may become partially masked through compensatory social strategies in individuals with high cognitive abilities.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "While neurodevelopmental etiology does not represent a separate DSM-5 diagnostic category, evaluating developmental course and etiological mechanisms is mandatory for assigning DSM-5 etiological specifiers across all neurodevelopmental diagnoses.\n\nClinicians must systematically evaluate: (1) prenatal and perinatal exposures (e.g., maternal valproate exposure, fetal alcohol spectrum, prematurity, low birth weight); (2) known genetic anomalies (e.g., Trisomy 21, Fragile X syndrome, 22q11.2 deletion syndrome, Rett syndrome MECP2 mutation); (3) family pedigree to determine heritability patterns; and (4) developmental milestone trajectories across gross motor, fine motor, expressive language, receptive language, and adaptive milestones.\n\nDocumenting these factors provides the evidence base for assigning the DSM-5 specifier: 'Associated with a known medical or genetic condition or environmental factor'.",
        'specifiers' => "Etiological specifiers include: 'Associated with a known genetic condition' (identifying chromosomal or molecular mutations such as FMR1 expansion in Fragile X); 'Associated with an environmental factor' (specifying prenatal teratogen exposure or neonatal hypoxia); and 'Associated with another neurodevelopmental, mental, or behavioral disorder' (e.g., recording comorbid epilepsy or sleep-wake disorders).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Clinicians must distinguish primary neurodevelopmental conditions from acquired neurological conditions (such as pediatric autoimmune neuropsychiatric disorders associated with streptococcal infections [PANDAS], anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, or progressive neurodegenerative leukodystrophies).\n\nIn acquired or neurodegenerative conditions, children exhibit a period of normal developmental milestone achievement followed by a documented regression or loss of motor, language, and cognitive skills, accompanied by focal neurological signs or systemic medical symptoms, which necessitates emergent neuroimaging and neurometabolic evaluation.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves a 3-year-old child who presents with severe language delay, hand-flapping stereotypies, and intellectual disability. Physical examination reveals prominent macroorchidism, large ears, and a long face. Microarray and molecular genetic testing confirm a CGG triplet repeat expansion (>200 repeats) in the FMR1 gene.\n\nThe developmental delays represent the behavioral phenotype of Fragile X syndrome. The diagnostic formulation records Autism Spectrum Disorder and Moderate Intellectual Disability, with the etiological specifier: 'Associated with a known genetic condition: Fragile X syndrome'.",
        'summary_notes' => "Key licensure board pearls: Neurodevelopmental disorders demonstrate high polygenic heritability alongside copy number variant risks. Fragile X syndrome is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability and autism spectrum presentations. Symptom presentations evolve dynamically across developmental stages; adult ADHD manifests primarily through executive dysfunction and internal restlessness rather than overt physical running and climbing.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Polygenic & CNV Architecture', 'icon' => '🧬', 'definition' => 'Etiology involves the cumulative impact of hundreds of common genetic variants alongside rare structural copy number variations.'],
            ['title' => 'Synaptic Pruning Perturbations', 'icon' => '✂️', 'definition' => 'Atypical developmental pruning leads to alterations in local vs. long-range neural connectivity across cortical networks.'],
            ['title' => 'Developmental Canalization', 'icon' => '🛤️', 'definition' => 'Early developmental disruptions alter subsequent neurodevelopmental trajectories, compounding downstream social and academic challenges.'],
            ['title' => 'Phenotypic Transformation', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Behavioral manifestations shift over time; for example, gross motor hyperactivity evolves into internal cognitive restlessness in adulthood.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Copy Number Variant (CNV)', 'definition' => 'A structural genomic variation involving duplications or deletions of sections of DNA that alter gene dosage and neurodevelopmental signaling.'],
            ['term' => 'Fragile X Syndrome', 'definition' => 'An X-linked genetic condition caused by FMR1 gene CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion, representing the leading inherited cause of intellectual disability.'],
            ['term' => 'Synaptogenesis', 'definition' => 'The rapid biological formation of synapses between neurons during early childhood brain maturation.'],
            ['term' => 'Neurodevelopmental Trajectory', 'definition' => 'The projected pathway of neurological and behavioral development an individual follows from infancy across the lifespan.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis & Comorbidity',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-comorbidity',
        'description' => 'Disentangling overlapping presentations: ADHD vs. ASD, SLD vs. Intellectual Disability, and anxiety mimicry.',
        'overview' => "A central challenge in child and adolescent psychopathology is navigating the high rate of clinical overlap and comorbidity within the neurodevelopmental spectrum. Shared behavioral phenotypes—such as poor attention span, emotional dysregulation, social friction, and academic underperformance—frequently obscure distinct diagnostic boundaries, leading to misdiagnosis or delayed therapeutic intervention.\n\nA classic diagnostic boundary involves distinguishing ADHD from Autism Spectrum Disorder. While children with ADHD may struggle in peer interactions due to impulsive interruption or missing social cues, they maintain intact capacity for joint attention and social-emotional reciprocity when engaged. In contrast, individuals with ASD exhibit fundamental qualitative impairments in the intuitive architecture of social-emotional reciprocity, alongside restricted interests and sensory atypicalities.\n\nSimilarly, anxiety disorders and trauma-related conditions can mimic neurodevelopmental inattention and restlessness. An anxious child hypervigilant to perceived threats may appear highly distractible in class, but the distraction is driven by intrusive anxious cognitions rather than the neurodevelopmental executive dysfunction that characterizes ADHD.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "To establish accurate differential diagnosis and assign comorbid diagnoses under DSM-5, clinicians must evaluate: (1) whether symptoms occur exclusively in specific contexts (suggestive of situational or adjustment reactions) versus persisting pervasively across all developmental domains; (2) whether cognitive and academic deficits are generalized across all spheres (indicating Intellectual Disability) or circumscribed to isolated academic domains with intact IQ (confirming Specific Learning Disorder).\n\nDSM-5 explicitly eliminated the DSM-IV exclusion rule that previously prevented co-diagnosing ADHD in an individual with Autism Spectrum Disorder. Under current DSM-5 guidelines, if an individual with ASD meets full diagnostic criteria for ADHD (inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity exceeding developmental expectations), both diagnoses must be coded concurrently.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians assign specifiers indicating comorbid conditions, such as: 'Comorbid Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder, Combined Presentation', 'With accompanying depressive disorder', or 'With accompanying anxiety disorder', detailing the primary versus secondary functional targets of intervention.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "ADHD vs. Pediatric Bipolar Disorder: ADHD is a chronic, non-episodic condition with early childhood onset, whereas Bipolar Disorder is episodic, marked by distinct cycles of elevated mood, grandiosity, decreased need for sleep without fatigue, and hypersexuality.\n\nIntellectual Disability vs. Specific Learning Disorder: Intellectual Disability involves subaverage intellectual reasoning and multi-domain adaptive failure; SLD features normal general intelligence with severe, localized academic processing deficits.\n\nOppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD) vs. ADHD: ODD features active defiance, vindictiveness, and intentional hostility toward authority, whereas ADHD noncompliance is secondary to forgetfulness, distractibility, and working memory failure.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical case involves an 8-year-old child referred for aggressive outbursts and severe inattention during class. Educators suspect ADHD. Clinical evaluation reveals that the child has normal motor control and working memory during one-on-one testing, but becomes agitated and inattentive specifically when loud alarms sound or when unstructured group tasks begin.\n\nFurther history reveals restricted dietary routines, intense fascination with mechanical train schedules, and a lifelong inability to sustain peer eye contact. Because the inattention and emotional distress stem from sensory overload and social communication deficits, the diagnosis is Autism Spectrum Disorder, Level 1, rather than primary ADHD.",
        'summary_notes' => "Essential board examination pearls: DSM-5 permits the simultaneous dual diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder and ADHD when criteria for both conditions are met. Always differentiate generalized adaptive impairment (Intellectual Disability) from circumscribed academic impairment (Specific Learning Disorder). Distinguish between primary neurodevelopmental executive dysfunction (ADHD) and cognitive interference secondary to anxiety or trauma hypervigilance.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Dual ASD and ADHD Diagnosis', 'icon' => '🤝', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 officially permits concurrent diagnosis of ASD and ADHD, reversing historical diagnostic prohibitions.'],
            ['title' => 'Generalized vs. Circumscribed Deficits', 'icon' => '🔍', 'definition' => 'Differentiating multi-domain adaptive/intellectual failure (ID) from isolated academic deficits with intact general IQ (SLD).'],
            ['title' => 'Anxiety-Driven Distraction', 'icon' => '😰', 'definition' => 'Distinguishing inattention caused by anxious cognitive rumination or trauma hypervigilance from organic executive dysfunction.'],
            ['title' => 'Episodic vs. Chronic Trajectory', 'icon' => '📈', 'definition' => 'Bipolar mood episodes are cyclical with distinct affective shifts, whereas ADHD symptoms are chronic and developmentally enduring.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Comorbidity', 'definition' => 'The simultaneous presence of two or more distinct clinical conditions in the same individual, each meeting formal DSM-5 criteria.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Overshadowing', 'definition' => 'The clinical error of attributing all presenting symptoms to a prominent known condition (e.g., Intellectual Disability) while overlooking a distinct comorbid treatable disorder.'],
            ['term' => 'Ego-Dystonic vs. Syntonic', 'definition' => 'Whether a symptom is experienced as alien, distressing, and inconsistent with the self-concept (dystonic) or acceptable and integrated (syntonic).'],
            ['term' => 'Phenocopy', 'definition' => 'A behavioral presentation that closely mimics a specific disorder but arises from distinct environmental, psychological, or medical etiologies.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Educational Case Scenarios',
        'slug' => 'educational-case-scenarios-neurodevelopmental',
        'description' => 'Multi-domain clinical evaluations, standardized testing interpretations, and diagnostic formulation workflows.',
        'overview' => "Clinical competency in neurodevelopmental psychopathology requires the systematic integration of multiple data sources: parent developmental interviews, teacher rating scales, direct behavioral observation, and psychometrically standardized cognitive and adaptive testing. Because no single biological marker establishes a neurodevelopmental diagnosis, clinical formulations depend on rigorous evidence synthesis and contextual analysis.\n\nA comprehensive evaluation must examine whether observed deficits represent pervasive neurodevelopmental impairments or secondary reactions to environmental stressors, educational mismatches, or sensory deficits. Standardized tools—such as the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC), the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales, and the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule (ADOS)—provide objective norms that must be interpreted in light of the child's cultural and linguistic background.\n\nThis educational module examines multi-step clinical decision trees, demonstrating how clinicians formulate differential hypotheses, navigate conflicting observer reports, and arrive at responsible DSM-5 diagnostic conclusions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "In formulating educational case reviews, clinicians must systematically verify: (1) documentation of developmental onset during childhood or adolescence; (2) cross-setting verification of functional impairment across home, school, and community environments; (3) ruling out medical and sensory etiologies through audiology and visual examinations; and (4) ensuring symptom counts meet exact DSM-5 thresholds.\n\nFormulations must explicitly state primary diagnoses, clinical specifiers, support tiers, and formal differential exclusions to construct a defensible clinical summary.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulation must record all relevant specifiers: severity grading (Mild, Moderate, Severe, Profound), support level (Levels 1–3), cognitive/language specifiers ('With accompanying language impairment'), and environmental/medical etiology specifiers.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Case analyses routinely weigh: ADHD vs. Pediatric Anxiety; Mild Intellectual Disability vs. Specific Learning Disorder; Social (Pragmatic) Communication Disorder vs. Autism Spectrum Disorder Level 1; and Tourette's Disorder vs. Chronic Motor Tic Disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "Educational Case Vignette: A 9-year-old child is referred for chronic disruptive classroom behavior. The teacher reports frequent vocal noises, blurting out answers, and leaving the desk. Parent interview reveals that the vocal noises began 2 years ago, consist of abrupt grunting sounds preceded by an uncomfortable chest sensation, and worsen during evening homework.\n\nDirect observation reveals that the classroom blurting occurs immediately following an observable neck jerk and throat grunt. Comprehensive cognitive testing reveals superior intellectual ability with no inattentive errors during testing. The formulation identifies that the vocal noises and blurting represent vocal and motor tics rather than ADHD impulsivity, establishing a diagnosis of Tourette's Disorder, with secondary school anxiety.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Accurate formulation requires corroboration across multiple independent informants (parents, teachers, clinical testing). Cross-setting symptom expression is mandatory for ADHD and ASD. Always evaluate premonitory sensory urges when investigating unusual repetitive vocalizations or motor disruptions.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Multi-Informant Triangulation', 'icon' => '📐', 'definition' => 'Integrating behavioral ratings from parents, educators, and direct standardized clinical testing to resolve diagnostic ambiguity.'],
            ['title' => 'Cross-Setting Verification', 'icon' => '🏫', 'definition' => 'Verifying that functional impairments persist across both academic and domestic settings to confirm neurodevelopmental etiology.'],
            ['title' => 'Premonitory Urge Assessment', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Differentiating involuntary tic vocalizations from ADHD impulsive verbal blurting through sensory history.'],
            ['title' => 'Defensible Formulation Architecture', 'icon' => '📝', 'definition' => 'Constructing clinical summaries that clearly delineate primary diagnoses, specifiers, support tiers, and formal exclusions.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales', 'definition' => 'A standardized psychometric instrument evaluating adaptive behavior across communication, daily living skills, socialization, and motor domains.'],
            ['term' => 'ADOS-2', 'definition' => 'Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule; a standardized semi-structured assessment of social interaction, communication, and play.'],
            ['term' => 'Informant Discrepancy', 'definition' => 'A frequent clinical phenomenon where parent and teacher symptom rating scales diverge, necessitating investigation into environmental triggers.'],
            ['term' => 'Ecological Validity', 'definition' => 'The extent to which clinical psychometric findings reflect real-world functioning and behavioral demands in everyday natural environments.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Domain Mastery & Board Review',
        'slug' => 'domain-mastery-board-review-neurodevelopmental',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of neurodevelopmental disorders, diagnostic thresholds, and high-yield board review pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit provides a comprehensive synthesis of the Neurodevelopmental Disorders domain under DSM-5 criteria, designed to consolidate diagnostic distinctions, duration thresholds, and high-yield principles for professional licensure examinations. Mastery of this domain requires an integrated understanding of how genetic architecture, neural maturational windows, and adaptive functioning intersect across clinical practice.\n\nNeurodevelopmental conditions are distinguished from adult-onset psychiatric disorders by their onset in the developmental period, their chronic non-episodic course, and their pervasive impact on foundational cognitive, communicative, motor, and social capabilities. A thorough review of diagnostic hierarchies—such as ASD superseding Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder, and Tourette's Disorder superseding Persistent Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder—ensures rapid and accurate diagnostic categorization on complex board examination vignettes.\n\nClinicians and board candidates must demonstrate fluency in DSM-5 dimensional severity tiers, age-of-onset thresholds, and the operational definitions of adaptive functioning domains, avoiding common pitfalls such as relying solely on IQ scores or overlooking dual ADHD-ASD comorbidities.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Summary of Core DSM-5 Diagnostic Thresholds:\n\n1. Intellectual Disability: Deficits in intellectual functioning (Criterion A) and adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, or practical domains (Criterion B), with onset during the developmental period (Criterion C). Severity determined by adaptive functioning, not IQ.\n\n2. Autism Spectrum Disorder: Deficits in all 3 social communication criteria (Criterion A) and at least 2 of 4 restricted/repetitive behavior criteria (Criterion B), with early developmental onset and support level grading (Levels 1–3).\n\n3. ADHD: At least 6 symptoms of inattention and/or 6 symptoms of hyperactivity-impulsivity for children (5 symptoms for individuals age 17+), persisting for >= 6 months across 2+ settings, with several symptoms present prior to age 12.\n\n4. Specific Learning Disorder: Persistent academic difficulties in reading, writing, or math for >= 6 months despite targeted intervention, with skills substantially below age expectations and normal general intelligence.\n\n5. Tourette's Disorder: Both multiple motor tics and >= 1 vocal tic persisting for > 1 year, with onset prior to age 18.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Severity specifiers for Intellectual Disability (Mild, Moderate, Severe, Profound); Support levels for ASD (Levels 1, 2, 3); Clinical presentations for ADHD (Combined, Predominantly Inattentive, Predominantly Hyperactive/Impulsive); and Academic domain specifiers for SLD (Reading, Written Expression, Mathematics).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key board examination differential pearls:\n• Intellectual Disability vs. SLD: ID involves generalized multi-domain cognitive and adaptive impairment; SLD involves isolated academic impairment with preserved general IQ.\n• ASD vs. Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder: ASD features restricted/repetitive behaviors or sensory atypicalities; SPCD features pragmatic language deficits with an absence of restricted/repetitive behaviors.\n• Tourette's vs. Persistent Motor/Vocal Tic Disorder: Tourette's requires both motor AND vocal tics; Persistent Tic Disorder requires motor OR vocal tics, but never both.\n• Childhood-Onset Fluency Disorder vs. Speech Sound Disorder: Fluency disorder involves rhythm, prolongations, and repetitions; Speech sound disorder involves phoneme articulation and intelligibility.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 17-year-old high school senior struggling with chronic disorganization, missed assignment deadlines, and difficulty focusing during 50-minute lectures. Records confirm these organizational difficulties date back to age 10 in elementary school, but overt motor hyperactivity was never observed.\n\nThe student exhibits 5 inattentive symptoms across school and home environments. General intelligence, social reciprocity, and reading mechanics are completely normal. The candidate accurately identifies ADHD, Predominantly Inattentive Presentation, satisfying the adult threshold (5 symptoms) and the expanded DSM-5 onset threshold (prior to age 12).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: Adaptive functioning governs Intellectual Disability severity. DSM-5 onset for ADHD is prior to age 12, with a 5-symptom threshold for ages 17+. Both motor AND vocal tics persisting >1 year are required for Tourette's. Comorbid diagnosis of ADHD and ASD is fully permitted under DSM-5. Specific Learning Disorder requires 6 months of persistent difficulty despite targeted educational intervention.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Board Review Diagnostic Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of duration thresholds, symptom counts, and onset criteria across all 5 major neurodevelopmental categories.'],
            ['title' => 'Hierarchy of Exclusion', 'icon' => '👑', 'definition' => 'Understanding which diagnoses supersede others (e.g., ASD excludes SPCD; Tourette excludes Chronic Motor Tic Disorder).'],
            ['title' => 'Lifespan Developmental Adaptations', 'icon' => '🌱', 'definition' => 'Recognizing age-specific criteria adjustments, including the adult 5-symptom ADHD rule and phenotypic symptom transformation.'],
            ['title' => 'Non-Psychometric Clinical Priority', 'icon' => '🎯', 'definition' => 'Prioritizing adaptive, contextual, and environmental functioning assessments over isolated numerical test scores.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Neurodevelopmental Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The overarching clinical domain encompassing disorders of brain development that manifest early in life and impact personal, social, and academic functioning.'],
            ['term' => 'Differential Threshold', 'definition' => 'The precise symptom count, chronological duration, or qualitative distinction required to establish one diagnosis over another.'],
            ['term' => 'Support Level 1, 2, 3', 'definition' => 'The DSM-5 dimensional severity classification for Autism Spectrum Disorder describing the degree of external support required.'],
            ['term' => 'Criterion A / Criterion B Split', 'definition' => 'The fundamental structural requirement in ASD requiring deficits across both social communication (Domain A) and repetitive behaviors (Domain B).']
        ]
    ]
];
